  "text": "umably, the process of solving this problem involves using Vieta's formulas, which relate the sum and product of the roots of a polynomial to its coefficients. In this case, we have two polynomials $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$, and we need to find the values of $a$, $b$, and $c$ that make the given relationships between the roots of $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ hold.\n\nFirst, let's write out the relationships between the roots of $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ using Vieta's formulas. For $P(x)$, we have:\n\n1. $r+s+t = -a$\n2. $rs+rt+st = b$\n3. $rst = -c$\n\nFor $Q(x)$, we have:\n\n1. $(r-2)+(s+5)+(t-1) = -a' \\Rightarrow r+s+t+4 = -a'$\n2. $(r-2)(s+5)+(r-2)(t-1)+(s+5)(t-1) = b'$\n3. $(r-2)(s+5)(t-1) = (rst) - 120$\n\nNotice that we have two equations that are similar to those for $P(x)$, but with one constant added to the right-hand side. We can use this information to set up equations involving $a$, $b$, and $c$. \n\nFor example, we can subtract equation 3 from equation 1 and equation 1 from equation 2 to get:\n\n1. $(3 + (s+5) + (t-1)) - 4 = -a' + a$\n2. $(r-2)(s+5)+(r-2)(t-1)+(s+5)(t-1) - (rst) = b' - b$\n\nSimplifying these equations, we get:\n\n1. $s+t = -2$\n2. $(r-2)(s+5)+(r-2)(t-1)+(s+5)(t-1) = 0$\n\nNow, we have two equations with two unknowns ($s+t$ and $(r-2)(s+5)+(r-2)(t-1)+(s+5)(t-1)$). We can use these equations to solve for $a$, $b$, and $c$.\n\nFor example, we can substitute the value of $s+t$ from the first equation into the second equation to get:\n\n$(r-2)(s+5)+(r-2)(t-1)+(s+5)(t-1) = 0$\n\nSubstituting $s+t = -2$, we get:\n\n$(r-2)(-2+5)+(r-2)(-2-1)+(s+5)(-2-1) = 0$\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n$(r-2)(3)+(r-2)(-3)+(s+5)(-3) = 0$\n\n$3r - 6 - 3r + 6 + 3s - 15 = 0$\n\n$3s - 15 = 0$\n\n$s = 5$\n\nNow, we can substitute this value of $s$ back into the equation $s+t = -2$ to get:\n\n$5 + t = -2$\n\n$t = -7$\n\nNow, we have the roots $r$, $s$, and $t$, as well as the relationships between them. We can use these relationships to solve for $a$, $b$, and $c$.\n\nFor example, we can substitute the value of $t$ into the equation $rst = -c$ to get:\n\n$rs(-7) = -c$\n\nSubstituting the values of $r$ and $s$ from the relationships between the roots, we get:\n\nas + b + 50 = -120\n\nas + b = -170\n\nSolving this equation with the previous relationships between the roots, we get:\n\n$(2 \\cdot 5 + 3 \\cdot (-7)) + b = -170$\n\n$-7 + b = -170$\n\n$b = -163$\n\nNow, we can substitute the values of $b$ and $s$ into the equation $ls + rt + st = b$ to get:\n\n$5l + rt + (-7t) = -163$\n\nSubstituting the values of $r$ and $t$ from the relationships between the roots, we get:\n\n$5l + (-2)l + 35 = -163$\n\n$l + 35 = -163$\n\n$l = -208$\n\nNow, we have the values of $a$, $b$, and $c$ that satisfy the given relationships between the roots of $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$. We can use these values to solve for $c$.\n\nSubstituting the values of $a$, $b$, and $c$ into the equation $rst = -c$, we get:\n\n5 \\times (-2) \\times (-7) = -c\n\n$70 = -c$\n\n$c = -70$\n\nTherefore, the only possible value for $c$ is $\\boxed{-70}$.",
